WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.747
I give this to you.

00:00:05.747 --> 00:00:07.455
I want you to have
this with my blessing.

00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:11.700
In goes the mustard.

00:00:19.774 --> 00:00:20.728
Oh yeah.

00:00:20.728 --> 00:00:23.020
Bread's a bit like toilet
roll at the moment, isn't it?

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.430
It's like gold dust, you've
got to hunt the stuf down.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:28.250
I was lucky enough to
get one of the cheapest

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.083
loaves I could find.

00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:31.375
I almost got a sense that
these were the kind of loaves

00:00:31.375 --> 00:00:34.040
that you tend to use in, in an
American style grilled cheese?

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:34.940
Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.260
It's got a reasonable
sugar content,

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.560
so it caramelises beautifully.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.990
Look, grilled cheese isn't
supposed to be artsy.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.660
This thing we're doing here,
this is like a freebie.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.200
I'm only doing this just
to improve your life.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.340
This thing is better
with a hangover,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.830
it's better with cheap cheese.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:55.880
You know how most great
artists want to leave something

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.430
for the future for posterity?

00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.990
I give this to you.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:01.977
I want you to have
this with my blessing.

00:01:01.977 --> 00:01:03.560
And you can pass it
onto your children

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.790
in the fullness of time.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.440
What sets it apart from a
sort of typical toasted cheese

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:07.940
sandwich?

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.070
Because I've had grilled cheese
when I've been to the States.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.870
And there's something
almost a bit

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.210
disgusting about it, which
makes it so delicious.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.590
It's all the things your body
wants, it's fat, it's salt,

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.090
it's dough, and it's all those
things pointing to one place.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.210
But here we're going to
improve it even better.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.250
So take your two slices of
bread and grab your mayonnaise.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.120
Smear it on both
pieces of bread.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.180
Heat up your pan.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.500
Do I put any fat into the pan?

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.300
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:32.800
No?

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.370
It's much more
exciting than that.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.680
This is absolutely
the sandwich that can

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.110
cure anything, a broken heart.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.530
Take a slice of bread, put it in
the pan, mayonnaise side down.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.910
Mayonnaise down on
to the un-oiled pan.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:46.650
Yep.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.440
Now, carefully put
the cheese on top.

00:01:51.423 --> 00:01:53.090
And put the second
piece of bread on top

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:54.860
of that, mayonaise side up.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:55.400
Got you.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.370
Good.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:57.683
A bit of cheese overboard.

00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:58.850
Doesn't matter, that's fine.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.130
Move the sandwich around to sort
of pick up the leaky cheese.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.700
So why mayonnaise on the
outside of the bread rather than

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.450
butter?

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.450
Basically, by using
mayonnaise what you're doing

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.229
is you're frying
the bread the same

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.330
as you would on a
regular grilled cheese.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:13.790
But you're also having a kind
of lovely delicious flavoured

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.740
omelety thing to it as well.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.690
I'm salivating
thinking about this.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:18.190
Yeah?

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.080
Yeah, I told you,
change your life.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:24.110
Flip it over and lick
any remaining mayonnaise

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:24.860
off your fingers.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.170
Is this the classic
American style?

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.580
They always put their
mayonnaise on the outside?

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.590
They don't always,
but the good ones do.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.340
And the cheese inside is
looking gooey and delicious.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.840
Move the sandwich around in the
oils and fats and greasy bits.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.472
so it picks up the last bits.

00:02:40.472 --> 00:02:41.930
It's looking like
it might be done,

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:44.397
the cheese is beautifully
melted inside.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.230
Put it onto a plate.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.073
If you want to,
you can lift it out

00:02:47.073 --> 00:02:49.490
and put it on to some kitchen
roll and dab the fat off it.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.782
Oh no, goodness no, that
would be a waste, wouldn't it?

00:02:51.782 --> 00:02:53.890
If you do, we cannot be friends.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:54.920
That's it forever.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.270
All over, man.

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.130
You are dead to me.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.470
Oh, the cheese is
starting to ooze out now.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.530
Oh, it's a good
feeling, isn't it?

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:04.160
Onto a plate.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.130
- Oh my goodness.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.900
The cheese is oozing out.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.100
So good.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.030
You could also think
of it like a steak.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.760
So you need it to rest
for a little while

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.718
as well, just for all
the little bits inside it

00:03:15.718 --> 00:03:21.260
to sort of become promiscuous,
as it were, and blend.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.380
And also, it's too bloody hot
to put any of it in your mouth.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:25.954
Knife straight through.

00:03:25.954 --> 00:03:26.898
Look at that.

00:03:30.674 --> 00:03:31.420
There it is.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.650
Oh mate, that's just
a thing of beauty.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.440
- Yeah?

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:39.940
Oh yeah.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.520
This is one of the
sandwiches for one

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.710
of those many long evenings
we've got ahead of us.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.710
Maybe feeling a little
bit blue about the months

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:55.388
ahead in isolation,
make one of these.

00:03:55.388 --> 00:03:56.930
Somebody said the
other day that they

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.840
don't feel like keeping some
of their older wines anymore.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.910
Life is too short to
put these things off.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:03.880
I've got a bottle
of Cote du Rhone

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.050
I found at the bottom
of the wine cellar,

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.760
and I'm going to drink it
with a grilled cheese sandwich

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.260
next week.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:15.710
We've got a little
time coming up

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.290
when we're going to have weird
and random ingredients coming

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.855
in, and quite a lot of time
spent in the kitchen, I hope.

00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:22.730
And what's nice about
something like bechemel

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.313
is it's probably the
first thing you'd

00:04:24.313 --> 00:04:25.460
learn at catering college.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.630
It's absolutely the simplest and
most basic tool to play with.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:33.710
Add some milk to the basic paste
and maybe some chicken stock

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.210
and you've got
premium chicken soup.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.200
If you add meat stock to it
you've got a thick dark gravy.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.220
We're actually going
to thicken some beer

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:43.220
to make the basis of the
sauce, which still have

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.170
a lot of cheese in it as well.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.660
But you can then use to
make a Welsh rarebit, which

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:50.060
is a brilliant snack, a
really, really easy thing.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.610
Quite a child pleaser, quite
a crowd pleaser as well.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.830
Stick that slice of
bread in the toaster.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.590
Just go ahead, not too dark.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.090
A lump of butter
here about the size

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.587
of a tablespoon for a base.

00:05:03.587 --> 00:05:04.420
Something like that?

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.770
No, smaller than that.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.240
Little smaller, half that.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:10.758
As that butter is melting,
add to it, roughly,

00:05:10.758 --> 00:05:11.800
the same amount of flour.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.632
Combine the two together.

00:05:14.632 --> 00:05:16.840
I'm going to put a spot more
butter in mine actually.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.507
More butter is not
going to hurt, is it?

00:05:18.507 --> 00:05:20.410
What sort of smell are
you getting off yours?

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:20.910
Good.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.000
Should I be adding more butter?

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.820
Mine is starting to
bubble up a little bit.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:24.990
Nope, bubbling is good.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:27.820
Important thing here is to
use a terrible, terrible chefy

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.190
term is to cook out the flour.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.067
How long would you
cook out the flour for?

00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:34.150
Until you get this kind
of biscuity smell, really.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.110
I'm getting it now,
rich tea biscuits.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.800
OK, so now open your beer.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.440
This is all I had lying
around, Newcastle Brown.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.050
Class act.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.360
So it will easily bubble up
and then go incredibly lumpy.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.140
So you add more beer and
keep stirring each time.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:50.857
So why beer?

00:05:50.857 --> 00:05:52.690
Why are we doing this
with beer and not milk

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.615
as I suspect people
would do normally.

00:05:54.615 --> 00:05:56.740
Because beer and cheese is
an amazing and delicious

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:57.850
combination.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:59.890
Mine's got like scrambled eggs.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.140
That's OK, keeps stirring.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.110
Is that more like it?

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:02.902
Yeah, that's great.

00:06:02.902 --> 00:06:06.180
OK, stick a couple of
teaspoons, at least,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.480
of English mustard in there.

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:08.830
I've got Dijon, is that OK?

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.240
Dijon will be absolutely fine.

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.395
Add a big shot of Worcester
sauce, topping your cheese.

00:06:13.395 --> 00:06:14.770
The other thing
you can do that's

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.860
really, really good with it
is put it in baked potatoes.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.320
So this time, you can also
add if you want to things

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.260
like Tabasco, black pepper.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.770
You've got a kind
of glue now, yeah?

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.050
Now you taste it and
see what else it needs.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:27.690
Mine needs more Worcester sauce.

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:29.710
I'm going to give
mine an Indian twist.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.190
I can't recommend this
stuff highly enough.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:36.910
I've always wanted to lay this
stuff out on grease proof paper

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.040
and let it go cold.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.790
And you could slice
it into squares

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:42.300
the same as that really
bad American burger cheese.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.790
Oh wow, this would be the
ultimate burger slice.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.290
This Guinness - and I know it's
the middle of the afternoon,

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:48.810
but we're in lockdown, mate.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:50.090
It's going to be done.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:51.130
It's almost like
Christmas, isn't it?

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.020
Being in lockdown, minus
the seasonal cheer.

00:06:55.630 --> 00:06:56.290
Good?

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:56.870
Oh yeah.

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:57.828
Let's mirror it on top.

00:06:57.828 --> 00:07:00.290
Right to the edge, but thicker
and heavier in the middle.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.373
Oh some of it seems to
have leaked onto my finger.

00:07:02.373 --> 00:07:03.890
Oh no.

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:04.390
Mmm.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.070
Shove it under your grill.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:09.040
Oh, I think I have saved
it in the knick of time.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.290
OK, I'm going to have to
show you a trade secret here.

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:13.330
Basically, you take a
knife, don't cut it,

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:15.807
just push down into the
cheese on the top like this.

00:07:15.807 --> 00:07:16.390
No, like this.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:17.320
Watch.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.960
One, two, three.

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:23.280
And then one, two, three.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.840
So you cross hatch
the top with grooves.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.962
That gives you a place for
the extra Worcester sauce

00:07:30.962 --> 00:07:32.420
to run in through
the cheesy cover.

00:07:36.303 --> 00:07:36.803
Mmmm.

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:44.530
What do you think?

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.850
I think this is the
most beautiful thing.

